{
 "schema": "bto-studio.storyboard.v1",
 "channel": "behind-the-ordinary",
 "topicId": "BTO-301",
 "slug": "why-qr-codes-have-three-corner-squares",
 "version": 2,
 "author": "visual-director skill, V2 direction after owner review of V1 (2026-10-20)",
 "treatment": {
  "educationalObjective": "The viewer leaves knowing that the three corner squares are position detection patterns, that a scanner finds them by reading rows of black and white and looking for a dark-light-dark-light-dark run of 1:1:3:1:1, that this ratio was chosen because it is rare in print, and that three found corners (with one empty) give the code's orientation.",
  "visualHook": "Frame 0 is a phone viewfinder already locked onto a real, scannable QR code printed on a tilted package, in perspective. On 'QR' every data module peels off towards the camera and blows away; the camera straightens to look straight down and only the three squares remain, with a question mark where a fourth corner would be.",
  "throughLine": "One real symbol, seen first as a product, then through the scanner's own eyes (rows of black and white), then as an object with layers, then as a measurement; the empty fourth corner planted in the hook is resolved in the payoff, and the last frame returns to the first.",
  "mechanismsToAnimate": [
   "what a scanner actually reads: one row of black and white runs at a time",
   "the 1:1:3:1:1 signature found exactly in the real code's rows",
   "the three stacked layers of one pattern, physically lifted in perspective",
   "look-alike squares in print failing the template",
   "invariance of the ratio as the code turns",
   "orientation from three corners plus one empty corner"
  ],
  "factualRisks": [
   "Row runs in the scanner-vision scene are computed from the real encoded matrix (segno); the 1:1:3:1:1 match is exact module counts, not an illustration.",
   "The layer stack is the real finder geometry; layer labels name what is visible (dark ring, light ring, dark core) and carry no dimensions.",
   "Ratio invariance is shown only for in-plane rotation, where it is exact; perspective tilt is used only for the packaging shots, never while measuring.",
   "Printed-matter samples and the business-form search are conceptual depictions (CONCEPTUAL chip); no survey data or frequencies are shown.",
   "The code encodes 'HIDDEN LOGIC' (no URL) and is decoded in QA."
  ]
 },
